article_spellcheck:
Our usually quiet town became quite animated on Tuesday afternoon by the arrival of several military companies who were handsomely received by our gallant Continentals. After passing round the square, they entered the area in front of the Court-House, where a felicitous address of welcome was delivered to them by CHARLES J. Brent, Esq. Other companies arrived on Wednesday morning, and the battalion, when formed, consisted of the following companies:

Continental Guards, Capt. WASHINGTON;

Hampshire Guards, Capt. Butler;

Jefferson Guards, Capt. ROWAN;

Alexandria Rifles, Capt. HEEBER;

Hampshire Guards, Capt. SHERRAD;

West Augusta Guards, Capt. BAYLOR;

Newtown Cavalry, Capt. DRAKE.

Several of these companies had one bands of music with them.

Col. L. MOORE, Lieut. Col. WM. DENNY and Maj. J. RELY, were the field officers in command. The whole made a soldierly and attractive appearance, and we trust the occasion has proved to be a pleasant one both to visitors and entertainers.—Winchester, R.P.

On Thursday night a grand Banquet was given by the Continental Guards, to the companies remaining in Winchester. A number of invited guests were present. Toasts were given which elicited speeches from Hon. Henry Bedinger, Capt. Baylor, of the Augusta Guards, Hon. J. J. Fauxner, A. X. BOTELER, Esq., Hon. James N. Mason, who happily alluded to the appropriateness of the name of the Morgan Continental Guards, in the town of Winchester, the town where his dust now moulders, and also the appropriateness of selection of Gen. Washington as the commander of the company. After Col. Lee had concluded, Capt. Washington acknowledged the compliment in handsome and appropriate remarks. Speeches were also made in response to toasts, by Lieutenant Lee, of the Hampshire Guards, C. Lewis Brent, Mr. Conrad, &c. The entertainment was concluded by humorous stories by the inimitable JOHN B. BLOSPELL, who convulsed his hearers with the dialogue of "Stookey" and himself, and also Mrs. Scott.

The entertainment, although hastily gotten up, reflected credit upon Capt. Washington and his ASSOCIATES of the Guards, and will long be remembered by all present.

The people of Winchester are hard to beat in acts of hospitality, every one doing everything in their power to make their visitors feel at home.

